<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pedro Álvares Cabral</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1467–c. 1520</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pedro Álvares Cabral was a Portuguese navigator who reached the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> in 1500 while leading a fleet to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. His landfall gave Portugal a claim to Brazil. Brazil became the most durable territorial possession of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pedro-alvares-cabral.jpg" alt="Pedro Álvares Cabral" width="284" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aurélio de Figueiredo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pedro_%C3%81lvares_Cabral.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pedro Álvares de Gouveia was born in 1467 or 1468, the former year being the more likely, at Belmonte in central Portugal. He was a son of Fernão Álvares Cabral and Isabel Gouveia, one of five boys and six girls. Little is certain about his life before 1500. He began to use his father's surname only later, supposedly after his elder brother's death in 1503.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 15 February 1500 Cabral was appointed capitão-mor of a fleet bound for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a>'s newly opened route around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. The aim was to return with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> and to open trade, bypassing the monopoly of Arab, Turkish and Italian merchants. The fleet of 13 ships left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> on 9 March 1500. On 23 March a nau commanded by Vasco de Ataíde, with 150 men, disappeared without trace. The fleet then sailed far to the west, and on 22 April 1500 anchored near what Cabral named Monte Pascoal, on the eastern coast of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>.</p><p>Cabral at first took the land for a large island, but after following its coast he became convinced it was a continent. It lay within the Portuguese sphere under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tordesillas">Treaty of Tordesillas</a> of 1494, and he claimed it for the Crown, sending a ship to inform King Manuel I. Around 5 May the fleet turned east. A storm in the South Atlantic on 23 or 24 May cost four ships, among them one commanded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolomeu-dias">Bartolomeu Dias</a>, and 380 men.</p><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a>, reached on 13 September, Cabral negotiated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamorin">Zamorin</a> and obtained permission to set up a factory. On 16 or 17 December the factory was attacked by some 300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> and Indians, or by several thousand according to other accounts, and more than 50 Portuguese were killed. Cabral looted and burned the Arab fleet and bombarded the city. He then allied with the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Cochin</a>, loaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, and set out for Portugal on 16 January 1501.</p><p>Manuel I chose Cabral to command a fleet to avenge the losses at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a>, but he was relieved for reasons that remain uncertain, possibly because he opposed giving Vicente Sodré independent command of part of it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> led the fleet that left in March 1502. Cabral left the court for good, and few records of his later life survive. His voyage was little remembered for over 300 years, until Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-ii-of-brazil">Pedro II of Brazil</a> sponsored research on it from the 1840s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pedro Álvares Cabral” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1494 Portugal and Castile divided the New World along the Tordesillas meridian, and on 22 April 1500 <b>Pedro Álvares Cabral</b>, leading 13 ships and more than 1,000 men on the route to India, landed at what is now Porto Seguro and took possession of the land for…</blockquote><a href="/empire/colonial-brazil">Colonial Brazil · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Pedro Álvares Cabral</b>’s visit in 1500 and Vasco da Gama’s demand for tribute in 1502, Francisco de Almeida took the town in July 1505 and installed a client sultan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kilwa-sultanate">Kilwa Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Vasco da Gama reached Calicut in 1498, <b>Pedro Álvares Cabral</b> reached Brazil in 1500, and by mid-century Portuguese traders were in China and Japan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pedro Álvares Cabral</b>’s landfall in Brazil in 1500 added an American territory that would become the empire’s most valuable possession.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The coast included the place where <b>Pedro Álvares Cabral</b> had landed in 1500.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-of-porto-seguro">Captaincy of Porto Seguro</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Belmonte, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santarem-portugal">Santarém</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.37,-7.35&amp;year=1501" aria-label="Belmonte on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4369 721 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pedro Álvares Cabral"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4819 991h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4819 991h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.37,-7.35&amp;year=1501">What was here in 1501? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
